* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌæsəˈteɪn/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|ăs'-ər-tānʹ}}, {{IPA|/ˌæsɚˈteɪn/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-ascertain.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪn|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|as|cer|tain|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[find out]] [[definitely]]; to [[discover]] or [[establish]].
#: {{syn|en|determine|discover|establish|find out|learn|work out}}
#: {{ux|en|As soon as we '''ascertain''' what the situation is, we can plan how to proceed.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1905|author=[[w:Emma Orczy|Baroness Emmuska Orczy]]
|title=[[w:The Case of Miss Elliott|The Tremarn Case]]
|chapter=1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL8479084W
|passage=“There the cause of death was soon '''ascertained''' ; the victim of this daring outrage had been stabbed to death from ear to ear with a long, sharp instrument, in shape like an antique stiletto, which […] was subsequently found under the cushions of the hansom.&nbsp;[…]”}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To make (someone) [[certain]] or [[confident]] about something; to [[inform]].
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1436|editor=Sir {{w|Nicholas Harris Nicolas}}|title=Proceedings and Ordinances of the Privy Council of England|volume=IV|year_published=1835|page=352c|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=v_w9AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA352-IA3&lpg=PA352-IA3|passage=Therfore the saide cõmissioners shall mowe say that nowe late during the parlement the King '''ascertaigned''' of the saide maliciouse p<sup>r</sup>pose of his enemys, willed and desired the lords being then present to shewe their̃ good willes aide and helpe for the saide rescues{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1844|origdate=1483|author=Caroline A. Halsted|quotee={{w|Richard III}}|chapter=XV|title=Richard III as Duke of Gloucester and King of England|page=307|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bViANRKjAX0C&pg=PA307&lpg=PA307|passage=We would most gladly ye came yourself if that ye may {{...}} praying you to '''ascertain''' us of your News.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1769|author=William Robertson|editor={{w|George Gleig}}|chapter=The History of the Reign of the Emperor Charles V|title=The Historical Works of William Robertson|volume=V|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Doig & Stirling|year_published=1813|page=395|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yrgQAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA395|passage=Muncer Assured them, that the design was approved of by Heaven, and that the Almighty had in a dream '''ascertained''' him of its success.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[establish]], to [[prove]].
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1791|author={{w|William Cowper}}|title={{w|Homer}}’s {{w|Odyssey}}|volume=II|part=book XI|page=274 (footnote)|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/iliadodysseyofho02home#page/274|passage=The two firſt lines of the following book ſeem to '''aſcertain''' the true meaning of the concluſion of this, and to prove ſufficiently that by [[Ωκεανός]] here, Homer could not poſſibly intend any other than a river.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1800|author={{w|Edward Malone}}|title=The Critical and Miscellaneous Prose Works of John Dryden|volume=III|location=London|publisher=H. Baldwin and Son|page=382 (note 8)|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7j0zAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA382|passage=In 1695 he &#91;{{w|Walter Moyle}}&#93; was chosen to represent the borough of Saltash in parliament ; a circumstance which '''ascertains''' the piece before us to have been written subsequent to that period.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1842|author={{w|Isaac D'Israeli}}|chapter=The Ship of Fools|title=Amenities of Literature: Consisting of Sketches and Characters of English Literature|volume=I|location=Paris|publisher=Baudry|page=254|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=I4dLAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA254|passage=We must look somewhat deeper would we learn why a book which now tries our patience was not undeserving of those multiplied editions which have '''ascertained''' its popularity.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[ensure]] or [[effect]].
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|date=1751-06-29|author={{w|Samuel Johnson}}|journal={{w|The Rambler}}|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=opgDAAAAQAAJ|volume=III|number=134|location=London|publisher=A. Millar et al.|year_published=1761|page=155|passage=It is true, that no diligence can '''aſcertain''' ſucceſs ; death may intercept the ſwifteſt career ; but he who is cut off in the execution of an honeſt undertaking, has at leaſt the honour of falling in his rank, and has fought the battle, though he miſſed the victory.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1757–65|author={{w|Tobias Smollet}}|chapter=X|title=The History of England from the Revolution to the Death of George II|volume=II|location=London|publisher=Richardson & Co.|year_published=1830|part=book I|page=224|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FSEwAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA224|passage=The ministry, in order to '''ascertain''' a majority in the house of lords, persuaded the queen to take a measure which nothing but necessity could justify.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|year=1824|author=Sir {{w|Walter Scott}}|chapter=Administration|title={{w|St. Ronan's Well}}|location=Boston|publisher=Samuel H. Parker|page=29|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Y3s_AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA29|passage=On the contrary, the Squire’s influence as a man of family and property, in the immediate neighbourhood, who actually kept greyhounds, and at least talked of hunters and races, '''ascertained''' him the support of the whole class of bucks, half and whole bred, from the three next counties ; and if more inducements were wanting, he could grant his favourites the privilege of shooting over his moors, which is enough to turn the head of a young Scotchman at any time.}}

